{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Who rules over all that is his.</b> For he saw that he was trustworthy in his entire household — therefore he trusted him that he would not take a bribe and take for his son a wife who is not fitting for him, for why would he take what is not worthy for him, seeing that he rules over all that is his? And we may say that the word 'his' (לוֹ) refers back to Eliezer himself, for everyone who runs after the lust of money — his money rules over him, guiding him wherever it pleases; but he does not rule over his money at all, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 6:2): ״אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יִתֵּן לוֹ הָאֱלֹהִים עֹשֶׁר וּנְכָסִים וְכָבוֹד״ and so on, ״וְלֹא יַשְׁלִיטֶנּוּ הָאֱלֹהִים לֶאֱכֹל מִמֶּנּוּ״ and so on. And it should have said 'a man whom the Elohim gave him' (אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לוֹ הָאֱלֹהִים), for 'יִתֵּן' conveys the future tense — and this is because the verse speaks of one who is not satisfied with his portion and always anticipates that HaShem will give him yet more beyond what is already in his hand, for he never has enough. And this is the man 'whom the Elohim does not enable to eat of it,' for his eye is evil against what is his own. But above this verse it says (Ecclesiastes 5:18): ״גַּם כָּל הָאָדָם אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לוֹ הָאֱלֹהִים עֹשֶׁר וּנְכָסִים וְהִשְׁלִיטוֹ לֶאֱכֹל מִמֶּנּוּ״ and so on, ״זֹה מַתַּת אֱלֹהִים הִיא״. There it mentions 'נָתַן' in the past tense — this is the one who is content and rejoices in his portion; he is the man who rules and has dominion over all that is his, and this one will certainly not take what is not worthy for him. And from this perspective he trusted Eliezer, for he knew of him that he rules over all that is his, and his money does not rule over him — therefore money would not compel him to do on its account what is not fitting and proper. And this is a precious interpretation." "<b>Please place your hand under my thigh.</b> He had him swear by the covenant of circumcision, for through circumcision a man is fenced off from forbidden sexual relations. Therefore he said, ״לֹא תִקַּח אִשָּׁה לִבְנִי מִבְּנוֹת הַכְּנַעֲנִי״ (Genesis 24:3), for sexual licentiousness is an inheritance among them from Ham, father of Canaan, for some say that he violated Noah (Sanhedrin 70a). Therefore it is not fitting that the circumcised one should be joined with practitioners of licentiousness, for through circumcision HaShem blessed Abraham, and through Ham's violation of Noah there came upon him ״אָרוּר כְּנָעַן״ (Genesis 9:25), and the blessed does not cleave to the cursed." "And what is said, ״וְאַשְׁבִּיעֲךָ בַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם וֵאלֹהֵי הָאָרֶץ״ — this is because the Sages said (Nedarim 31b): circumcision is great, for were it not for circumcision, heaven and earth would not have endured, as it is said (Jeremiah 33:25): ״אִם לֹא בְרִיתִי יוֹמָם וָלָיְלָה, חֻקּוֹת שָׁמַיִם וָאָרֶץ לֹא שָׂמְתִּי״. Therefore in the oath of circumcision he mentioned ״אֱלֹהֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם וְהָאָרֶץ״. And the reason for this matter is that circumcision completes the name Shaddai, and by that name the Holy One, blessed be He, said to His world 'enough' (דַּי), when the heavens and the earth were extending and expanding and going forth, as is found in the chapter 'Ein Dorshin' (Ḥagigah 12a). And through this, the statement that 'heaven and earth were not sustained' is reconciled — this implies that after they were created they could not endure; but the verse says 'I did not set' (לֹא שָׂמְתִּי) — which implies from the outset. Rather, since it stands on ״חֻקּוֹת שָׁמַיִם וָאָרֶץ״, on the decree given to them that they should not extend without limit — and the Holy One, blessed be He, rebuked them by the name Shaddai, for by it He said to His world 'enough' (דַּי). And were it not for this, they could not have endured if they had extended without limit — and all this came about through circumcision, which completes this name. But ״כְּשֶׁלְּקָחַנִי מִבֵּית אָבִי״ (Genesis 24:7) — this was before circumcision — He was ״אֱלֹהֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם״ alone, for the name Shaddai did not extend among the lower realms. And some say that the Holy One, blessed be He, is called ״אֱלֹהֵי הָאָרֶץ״ specifically in the land of Israel, for one who dwells outside the land and so on (Ketubot 110b), and as it is said (II Kings 17:26): ״לֹא יָדְעוּ אֶת מִשְׁפַּט אֱלֹהֵי הָאָרֶץ״. Therefore concerning outside the land he said ״ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם אֲשֶׁר לְקָחַנִי מִבֵּית אָבִי״ (Genesis 24:7), but here, where he stood in the land of Israel, he said ״בַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם וֵאלֹהֵי הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 24:3)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {2 "The Talmudic abbreviation כו׳ in the Ketubot citation stands for the well-known phrase כאילו אין לו אלוהָ — rendered here as 'and so on.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:46.727762046Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}